{"text":"Meta’s [HSTU reference configuration](https://github.com/meta-recsys/generative-recommenders/blob/main/configs/ml-20m/hstu-sampled-softmax-n128-final.gin) uses L2-normalized user/item embeddings, temperature 0.05 and 128 negatives—a reference point, not an Amazon optimum. With normalized representations, logits are cosine similarity divided by temperature; normalization prevents embedding norms from implicitly changing loss scale. Lower temperature concentrates gradients on high-scoring negatives and increases sensitivity to false negatives. Positive temperature scaling alone preserves inference ranking; normalizing item embeddings can change it. More negatives increase computation and denominator coverage, but gains depend on their distribution; [adaptive sampled-softmax research](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/blanc18a.html) finds proposals closer to model probabilities can reduce estimation bias with fewer samples.

Distinguish uncorrected candidate-set CE from importance-corrected sampled softmax. Uniform candidate CE learns discrimination against uniform distractors; model-dependent mining without correction changes that objective toward difficult competitors, rather than merely accelerating the same estimator. Hard-negative benefits for top-K ranking have theoretical support under [BPR/OPAUC assumptions](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.03472), not a general guarantee for HSTU CE. LogQ correction requires the actual conditional proposal or expected sample counts, accounting for exclusion, duplicates and sampling design. A deterministic top-k pool’s original uniform probabilities are insufficient; zero-support selection cannot recover catalog contributions. Corrected sampled softmax still generally has finite-sample bias, and the always-included positive needs distinct treatment ([derivation](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/blanc18a/blanc18a.pdf)).

Recommended ablations: fix HSTU, splits, seed and training schedule; vary normalization, temperature and negative count under uniform CE first. Then hold retained count and loss fixed while varying mining-pool size, hardness and uniform mixing; separately compare corrected versus uncorrected stochastic proposals. Report macro validation HR@10, per-category results, gradient/logit scales and GPU cost; include compute-matched comparisons."}
